===Israeli–Palestinian conflict===
<!-- WHEN ADDING CONTENT TO THIS SECTION, USE SHORTENED FOOTNOTES, RELIABLE SOURCES (WP:RSP), AND AVOID INCLUDING BIASED MATERIAL. DO NOT COPY AND PASTE QUOTATIONS. NOTE HOW THE NEW YORK TIMES IS ITALICIZED AND MENTIONED IN PREVIOUS PARAGRAPHS. -->

Since 2003, studies analyzing coverage of the [[Israeli–Palestinian conflict]] in the ''New York Times'' have demonstrated a bias [[Anti-Palestinianism|against Palestinians]] and in favor of Israel.{{Efn|A 2003 study in the ''[[Harvard International Journal of Press/Politics]]'' concluded that ''The New York Times'' reporting was more favorable to Israelis than to Palestinians.<ref name="viser">{{cite journal |last=Viser |first=Matt |date=September 2003 |title=Attempted Objectivity: An Analysis of the New York Times and Ha'aretz and their Portrayals of the Palestinian-Israeli Conflict |journal=The International Journal of Press/Politics |volume=8 |issue=4 |pages=114–120 |doi=10.1177/1081180X03256999 |s2cid=145209853 |quote=This study explores the biases, pro-Israeli and pro-Palestinian, by looking at quantitative indicators of news coverage in ''The New York Times'' and ''Ha'aretz''. Several time periods were examined (1987–88, 2000–01, and post-September 11, 2001), using multiple indicators. By these measures, ''The New York Times'' is more favorable toward the Israelis than the Palestinians, and the partiality has become more pronounced with time.}}</ref> A 2002 study published in the journal ''[[Journalism (journal)|Journalism]]'' examined Middle East coverage of the [[Second Intifada]] over a one-month period in ''The New York Times'', ''[[The Washington Post]]'' and the ''[[Chicago Tribune]]''. The study authors said that the ''Times'' was "the most slanted in a pro-Israeli direction" with a bias "reflected...in its use of headlines, photographs, graphics, sourcing practices, and lead paragraphs."<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Zelizer |first1=Barbie |last2=Park |first2=David |last3=Gudelunas |first3=David |date=December 2002 |title=How Bias Shapes the News: Challenging the New York Times' Status as a Newspaper of Record on the Middle East |journal=Journalism |language=en |volume=3 |issue=3 |pages=283–307 |doi=10.1177/146488490200300305 |s2cid=15153383}}</ref> A ''[[Media, War & Conflict]]'' study based on a quantitative analysis of use of [[Active voice|active]] and [[Passive voice|passive]] voice and of the sentiment of the language used during the [[First Intifada|first]] and [[Second Intifada|second]] Palestinian intifadas found the paper's coverage of the events was disproportionately anti-Palestinian and that such bias worsened from the First Intifada to the Second.<ref>{{cite journal |last=Jackson |first=Holly M |year=2023 |title=The New York Times distorts the Palestinian struggle: A case study of anti-Palestinian bias in US news coverage of the First and Second Palestinian Intifadas |journal=Media, War & Conflict |language=en |volume=17 |issue=1 |pages=116–135 |doi=10.1177/17506352231178148}}</ref>
<br>
A 2024 piece in the journal ''[[Israel Affairs]]'' also found "errors, omissions, and poor editorial supervision" in the paper's coverage of the Gaza war, arguing: 
:''The casualty reports throughout the war relied almost solely on the Gaza Ministry of Health, which is controlled by Hamas, and frequently produced false and inflated data. Also, the repeated omissions of IDF statements about journalists that were active Hamas operatives showed that the Times preferred to believe Aljazeera, a questionable news organisation serving an autocratic leader, over the credibility of the IDF, a formal institution of a democratic state.''<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Gilboa |first1=Eytan |last2=Sigan |first2=Lilac |date=September 2, 2024 |title=The New York Times coverage of the Israel-Hamas war: errors, omissions, and poor editorial supervision |journal=Israel Affairs |volume=30 |issue=5 |pages=939–957 |doi=10.1080/13537121.2024.2394292 |issn=1353-7121 |doi-access=free}}</ref>
[[Benjamin Netanyahu]] and others have also accused ''The New York Times'' of bias against Israel, accusations that [[Eric Alterman]] has deemed false.{{Sfn |Alterman |2023}} }}

==== Gaza war ====
{{Further|Media coverage of the Gaza war#The New York Times|Screams Without Words|The Silence That Meets the Rape of Palestinians}}
<!-- WHEN ADDING CONTENT TO THIS SECTION, USE SHORTENED FOOTNOTES, RELIABLE SOURCES (WP:RSP), AND AVOID INCLUDING BIASED MATERIAL. DO NOT COPY AND PASTE QUOTATIONS. NOTE HOW THE NEW YORK TIMES IS ITALICIZED AND MENTIONED IN PREVIOUS PARAGRAPHS. -->

''The New York Times'' has received criticism for its coverage of the [[Gaza war]] and [[Gaza genocide|genocide]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=Paul |first=Ari |date=August 4, 2025 |title=NYT Suppressed Genocide Discussion When It Could Have Made a Difference |url=https://fair.org/home/nyt-suppressed-genocide-discussion-when-it-could-have-made-a-difference/ |access-date=October 7, 2025 |website=FAIR |language=en-US}}</ref>{{Sfn |Folkenflik |2024}} In April 2024, ''[[The Intercept]]'' reported that a November 2023 internal memorandum by Susan Wessling and [[Philip Pan]] instructed journalists to reduce using the terms "genocide" and "ethnic cleansing" and to avoid using the phrase "[[Israeli-occupied territories|occupied territory]]" in the context of Palestinian land, "[[Palestine]]" except in rare circumstances, and the term "[[Palestinian refugee camps|refugee camps]]" to describe areas of Gaza despite recognition from the United Nations.{{Sfn |Scahill |Grim |2024A}} A spokesperson from the ''Times'' stated that issuing guidance was standard practice. An analysis by ''The Intercept'' noted that ''The New York Times'' described Israeli deaths as a massacre nearly sixty times, but had only described Palestinian deaths as a massacre once.{{Sfn |Scahill |Grim |2024A}} Writers and editors have left the newspaper due to its coverage of events in Gaza, including [[Jazmine Hughes]] and [[Jamie Lauren Keiles]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=Monteil |first=Abby |date=November 17, 2023 |title=Three LGBTQ+ Writers and Editors Have Left the 'New York Times' Over Its Coverage of Gaza |url=https://www.them.us/story/lgbtq-writers-editors-resigned-new-york-times-over-gaza-coverage |access-date=May 27, 2025 |website=Them |language=en-US}}</ref>

In December 2023, ''The New York Times'' published an investigation titled "[[Screams Without Words|'Screams Without Words': How Hamas Weaponized Sexual Violence on Oct. 7]]", alleging that [[Hamas]] weaponized [[Sexual and gender-based violence in the 2023 Hamas-led attack on Israel|sexual and gender-based violence]] during its [[2023 Hamas-led attack on Israel|armed incursion on Israel]].{{Sfn |Al Jazeera |2023}} The investigation was the subject of an article from ''The Intercept'' questioning the journalistic acumen of [[Anat Schwartz]], a filmmaker involved in the inquiry who had no prior reporting experience and agreed with a post stating Israel should "violate any norm, on the way to victory", doubting the veracity of the opening claim that Gal Abdush was raped in a timespan disputed by her family, and alleging that the ''Times'' was pressured by the [[Committee for Accuracy in Middle East Reporting in America]].{{Sfn |Scahill |Grim |Boguslaw |2024F}} ''The New York Times'' initiated an inquiry into the leaking of confidential information about the report to other outlets, which received criticism from [[NewsGuild-CWA|NewsGuild]] of New York president Susan DeCarava for purported racial targeting;{{Sfn |Wagner |2024a}} the ''Times''{{'}}s investigation was inconclusive, but found gaps in the way proprietary journalistic material is handled.{{Sfn |Bruell |2024}}

[[The New York Times Building]] has been a site of protest action during the Gaza war and [[Gaza genocide|genocide]], including a November 2023 sit-in demanding that ''The Times''{{'s}} editorial board publicly call for a ceasefire and accusing the media company of "complicity in laundering genocide",<ref>{{cite web |last1=Offenhartz |first1=Jake |date=November 10, 2023 |title=Protesters stage sit-in at New York Times headquarters to call for cease-fire in Gaza |url=https://apnews.com/article/new-york-times-protest-gaza-ceasefire-c1482efbb3f767a0898a3d20f9647bc2 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231110014425/https://apnews.com/article/new-york-times-protest-gaza-ceasefire-c1482efbb3f767a0898a3d20f9647bc2 |archive-date=November 10, 2023 |access-date=November 10, 2023 |website=Associated Press}}</ref> a February 29, 2024, protest and press conference following the release of ''The Intercept''{{'}}s critical investigation into the "Screams Without Words" exposé,<ref>{{Cite web |title=Video: Antiwar Activists Accuse The New York Times of 'Manufacturing Consent' for Genocide in Gaza |url=https://indypendent.org/2024/03/video-activists-accuse-the-new-york-times-of-manufacturing-consent-for-genocide-in-gaza/ |access-date=April 3, 2024 |website=[[The Indypendent]] |language=en-US}}</ref> and an [[Direct action|action]] on July 30, 2025, in which protesters spray-painted "NYT Lies, Gaza dies" on the building's glass facade.<ref>{{Cite news |last1=Marcius |first1=Chelsia Rose |date=September 29, 2025 |title=Police Arrest 3 in Connection With Vandalism of New York Times Building |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2025/09/29/nyregion/new-york-times-building-vandalism-charges.html |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251001142124/https://www.nytimes.com/2025/09/29/nyregion/new-york-times-building-vandalism-charges.html |archive-date=October 1, 2025 |access-date=October 7, 2025 |work=The New York Times |language=en}}</ref> In addition, protesters blocked ''The New York Times''{{'s}} distribution center March 14, 2024<ref>{{cite web |title=Pro-Palestine protesters block New York Times distribution centre |url=https://www.aljazeera.com/news/liveblog/2024/3/14/israels-war-on-gaza-live-rafah-attack-coming-soon-despite-pressure?update=2772435 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240316230237/https://www.aljazeera.com/news/liveblog/2024/3/14/israels-war-on-gaza-live-rafah-attack-coming-soon-despite-pressure?update=2772435 |archive-date=March 16, 2024 |access-date=March 16, 2024 |website=Al Jazeera}}</ref> and executive editor [[Joseph Kahn (journalist)|Joseph Kahn's]] residence was splattered with red paint on August 25, 2025.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Gainor |first=Danya |date=August 30, 2025 |title=Top New York Times editor's apartment building vandalized with paint and graffiti |url=https://www.cnn.com/2025/08/29/us/new-york-times-joseph-kahn-apartment-vandalism |access-date=October 7, 2025 |website=CNN |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=August 30, 2025 |title=Apartment building of New York Times executive editor vandalized with red paint |url=https://www.nbcnews.com/news/us-news/building-new-york-times-editor-vandalized-rcna228192 |access-date=October 7, 2025 |website=NBC News |language=en}}</ref> The collective [[Writers Against the War on Gaza]], which publishes the mock publication ''[[The New York War Crimes]]'', has been associated with protests against ''The New York Times''.<ref>{{Cite web |date=April 17, 2024 |title=Stealing the Voice of Authority {{!}} Arielle Isack |url=https://thebaffler.com/latest/stealing-the-voice-of-authority-isack |access-date=October 7, 2025 |website=The Baffler |language=en-US}}</ref> On October 27, 2025, 300 writers—including scholars, journalists, and public intellectuals—pledged<ref>{{Cite web |title=The New York Times: Boycott, Divest, Unsubscribe |url=https://www.boycottdivestunsubscribe.com/ |access-date=October 28, 2025 |website=The New York Times: Boycott, Divest, Unsubscribe |language=en-US}}</ref> to boycott ''The New York Times'' and withhold contributions to the paper in protest of what they describe as its complicity in the Gaza genocide, demanding 1) a review of anti-Palestinian bias in the newsroom, 2) a retraction of "Screams Without Words", and 3) a call from the editorial board for a US arms embargo on Israel.<ref name="Folta-2025">{{Cite web |last=Folta |first=James |date=October 27, 2025 |title=300+ pledge to boycott the New York Times' op-ed page over their anti-Palestinian bias. |url=https://lithub.com/300-pledge-to-boycott-the-new-york-times-op-ed-page-over-their-anti-palestinian-bias/ |access-date=October 28, 2025 |website=Literary Hub |language=en}}</ref><ref name="Middle East Eye">{{Cite web |title=Over 150 New York Times contributors to boycott paper over Gaza coverage |url=https://www.middleeasteye.net/news/over-150-new-york-times-contributors-say-they-will-boycott-paper-over-gaza-coverage |access-date=October 28, 2025 |website=Middle East Eye |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |last1=Adelson |first1=Daniel |last2=York |first2=New |date=October 27, 2025 |title=About 300 public figures announce boycott of New York Times over 'biased coverage' in Gaza |url=https://www.ynetnews.com/article/xi39njo9h |access-date=October 28, 2025 |work=Ynetglobal |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |last=Dayan |first=Linda |date=October 28, 2025 |title=Public Figures Demand The New York Times Retract Reporting on Sexual Assault on Oct. 7 |url=https://www.haaretz.com/us-news/2025-10-28/ty-article/.premium/genocide-is-not-an-opinion-a-listers-boycott-the-times-over-anti-palestinian-bias/0000019a-2aa5-ddf1-a1db-fefd1e130000 |work=[[Haaretz]]}}</ref> Among the initial signatories, about 150 had previously contributed to the ''Times''.<ref name="Folta-2025" /><ref name="Middle East Eye" />

===Transgender people===
{{See also|2020s anti-LGBTQ movement in the United States|Persecution of transgender people under the second Trump administration}}
''The New York Times'' has received criticism regarding its coverage of [[transgender]] people. When it published an opinion piece by [[Weill Cornell Medicine]] professor [[Richard A. Friedman]] called "How Changeable Is Gender?" in August 2015,{{Sfn |The New York Times |2015c}} ''[[Vox (website)|Vox]]''{{'}}s German Lopez criticized Friedman as suggesting that parents and doctors might be right in letting children suffer from severe dysphoria in case something changes down the line, and as implying that conversion therapy may work for transgender children.{{Sfn |Lopez |2015}} 

In February 2023, nearly one thousand{{Sfn |Oladipo |2023}} current and former ''Times'' writers and contributors wrote an open letter addressed to standards editor Philip B. Corbett, criticizing the paper's coverage of transgender, [[Non-binary gender|non⁠-⁠binary]], and [[gender-nonconforming]] people; some of the ''Times''{{'s}} articles have been cited in state legislatures attempting to justify criminalizing gender-affirming care.{{Sfn |Klein |2023a}} Contributors wrote in the open letter that "the ''Times'' has in recent years treated [[gender variance|gender diversity]] with an eerily familiar mix of [[pseudoscience]] and euphemistic, charged language, while publishing reporting on [[transgender youth|trans children]] that omits relevant information about its sources."{{Efn|Attributed to multiple references: {{Sfn |Migdon |2023}}{{Sfn |Yurcaba |2023}}{{Sfn |Kalish |2023}}{{Sfn |Yang |2023}}}} 

According to former ''Times'' journalist Billie Jean Sweeney, a push for writers to challenge "every aspect of being trans", ranging from gender-inclusive language to access to medical care, came from the top in 2022 after leadership was handed over to [[A. G. Sulzberger]], [[Joe Kahn]], and [[Carolyn Ryan]]; as part of an effort to win good will with the Trump campaign without incurring backlash from the general populace.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Monteil |first=Abby |date=January 2, 2026 |title=Former New York Times Editor Says Paper's Anti-Trans Slant Came From the Top |url=https://www.them.us/story/new-york-times-anti-trans-coverage-management-billie-jean-sweeney |access-date=January 7, 2026 |website=Them |language=en-US}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Hansford |first=Amelia |date=January 2, 2026 |title=Ex-New York Times editor claims senior staff are 'militantly' anti-trans |url=https://www.thepinknews.com/2026/01/02/new-york-times-trans-militant/ |access-date=January 7, 2026 |website=[[PinkNews]] |language=en-US}}</ref> 

The ''Times'' has continually denied any bias in its reporting, stating that its coverage of "fiercely contested medical and legal debates" is fair and balanced, and that it would not tolerate journalists protesting its transgender coverage.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Hansford |first=Amelia |date=January 6, 2026 |title=The New York Times insists trans reporting is fair and accurate |url=https://www.thepinknews.com/2026/01/06/new-york-times-insists-trans-reporting-accurate/ |access-date=January 7, 2026 |website=PinkNews {{!}} Latest lesbian, gay, bi and trans news {{!}} LGBTQ+ news |language=en-US}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=January 5, 2026 |title=Fact-Checking False Claims About Our Gender Identity Coverage |url=https://www.nytco.com/press/fact-checking-false-claims-about-our-gender-identity-coverage/ |access-date=January 7, 2026 |website=The New York Times Company |language=en-US}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=February 16, 2023 |title=NYT editors: Paper 'will not tolerate' its journalists protesting coverage of transgender people |url=https://thehill.com/homenews/3862101-nyt-editors-paper-will-not-tolerate-its-journalists-protesting-coverage-of-transgender-people/ |access-date=January 7, 2026 |website=The Hill |language=en-US}}</ref> In June 2026, civil rights attorney [[Alejandra Caraballo]] published a study in her newsletter, ''The Dissident'',<ref>{{cite web |date=17 June 2026 |title=Alejandra Caraballo |url=https://www.damemagazine.com/author/alejandra-caraballo/ |access-date=25 June 2026 |website=Dame}}</ref> which analyzed 3,242 articles that were published in the ''Times'' between 2014 and 2026.<ref>{{cite news |last=Caraballo |first=Alejandra |date=19 June 2026 |title=Exclusive: How The New York Times Changed Its Coverage of Trans People |url=https://www.thedissident.news/exclusive-how-the-new-york-times-changed-its-coverage-of-trans-people/ |accessdate=25 June 2026 |work=The Dissident}}</ref> Caraballo's study concluded that from 2022 onwards, the ''Times'' changed the way it covered transgender issues, "moving from rights-based framing toward more skeptical, conflict-driven coverage" which also prioritized the views of opponents of transgender rights ahead of the lived experience transgender people themselves.<ref>{{cite news |last=Wiggins |first=Christopher |date=21 June 2026 |title=The New York Times helped turn trans rights into political controversy, analysis finds |url=https://www.advocate.com/politics/national/new-york-times-transgender-controversy |accessdate=25 June 2026 |work=[[The Advocate (magazine)|The Advocate]]}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last=St. Palmer |first=Poppy-Jay |date=25 June 2026 |title=Study claims New York Times helped create political controversy over trans rights |url=https://www.thepinknews.com/2026/06/25/new-york-times-trans-reporting/ |accessdate=25 June 2026 |work=[[PinkNews]]}}</ref>

